        #revision
          %p This page collects material related to the article
          %ul
            %li#Cantor1895
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Cantor"}> Georg Cantor
                ,
              %p
                %em> Beiträge zur Begründung der transfiniten Mengenlehre
                ,
              %p Math. Ann. 46 (1895) pp.481-512,
              %p reprinted from p. 282 on in
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ernst+Zermelo"} Ernst Zermelo
                (ed.),
              %p
                %em> Georg Cantor – Gesammelte Abhandlungen mathematischen und philosophischen Inhalts
                ,
              %p Springer Berlin 1932
              %p
                (
                %a{:href => "https://archive.org/details/contributionstof00cant"}> online English translation
                )
          %p
            which is the origin of the modern concept of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinality"} cardinality
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"} William Lawvere
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cohesive+Toposes+and+Cantor%27s+%22lauter+Einsen%22"} Cohesive Toposes and Cantor's "lauter Einsen"
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#section_1_the_conception_of_power_or_cardinal_number"} Section 1. The conception of Power or Cardinal Number**
          %h2#section_1_the_conception_of_power_or_cardinal_number Section 1. The conception of Power or Cardinal Number**
          %p
            By an “aggregate” (Menge) we are to understand any collection into a whole (Zusammenfassung zu einem Ganzen)
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_1.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            of definite and separate objects of our intuition or our thought. These objects are called the “elements” of
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_2.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
          %p
            (i)
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_3.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M = \{m\}
          %p
            We denote the uniting of many aggregates
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_4.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_5.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_6.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_7.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ⋯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdots
          %p
            (2)
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_8.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %mi N
                    %mo ,
                    %mi P
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (M, N, P, \cdots)
          %p
            The elements of this aggregate are, therefore, the elements of
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_9.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_10.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_11.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_12.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_1
            whose elements are also elements of
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_13.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_14.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_2
            is a part of
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_15.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_1
            and
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_16.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_1
            is a part of
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_17.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_18.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_2
            is a part of
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_19.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
          %p
            Every aggregate
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_20.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            has a definite “power”, which we will also call its “cardinal number”.
          %p
            We will call by the name “power” or “cardinal number” of
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_21.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            the general concept which, by means of our active faculty of thought, arises from the aggregate
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_22.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            when we make abstraction of the nature of its various elements
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_23.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
            and of the order in which they are given.
          %p
            [482] We denote the result of this double act of abstraction, the cardinal -number or power of M, by (3)
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_24.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mover
                      %mi M
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{\overline{M}}
          %p
            Since every single element
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_25.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_26.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            is a definite aggregate composed of units, and this number has existence in our mind as an intellectual image or projection of the given aggregate
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_27.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_28.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            and
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_29.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            are “equivalent,” in signs
          %p
            (4)
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_30.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo ∼
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M \sim N
            or
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_31.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo ∼
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N \sim M
          %p
            if it is possible to put them, by some law, in such a relation to one another that to every element of each one of them corresponds one and only one element of the other. To every part
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_32.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_1
            of
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_33.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            there corresponds, then, a definite equivalent part
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_34.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_1
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_35.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
          %p
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_36.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
            …
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_37.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
          %p Every aggregate is equivalent to itself
          %p
            (5)
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_38.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo ∼
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M \sim M
          %p If two aggregates are equivalent to a third, they are equivalent to one another ; that is to say :
          %p
            (6) from
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_39.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo ∼
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M \sim P
            and
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_40.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo ∼
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N \sim P
            follows
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_41.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mo ∼
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M \sim N
          %p
            Of fundamental importance is the theorem that two aggregates
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_42.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            and
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_43.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            have the same cardinal number if, and only if, they are equivalent : thus,
          %p
            (7) from
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_44.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo ∼
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M \sim N
            we get
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_45.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mover
                        %mi M
                        %mo ¯
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo =
                    %mover
                      %mover
                        %mi N
                        %mo ¯
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{\overline{M}} = \overline{\overline{N}}
          %p and
          %p
            (8) from
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_46.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mover
                      %mi M
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo ¯
                  %mo =
                  %mover
                    %mover
                      %mi N
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{\overline{M}} = \overline{\overline{N}}
            we get
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_47.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mo ∼
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M \sim N
          %p Thus the equivalence of aggregates forms the necessary and sufficient condition for the equality of their cardinal numbers.
          %p
            [483] In fact, according to the above definition of power, the cardinal number
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_48.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            remains unaltered if in the place of each of one or many or even all elements
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_49.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
            of
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_50.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            other things are substituted. If, now,
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_51.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mo ∼
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M \sim N
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_52.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            and
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_53.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            are uniquely and reciprocally referred to one another; and by it to the element
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_54.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
            of
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_55.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            corresponds the element
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_56.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_57.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_58.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_59.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_60.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            of
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_61.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            substituted, and, in this way,
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_62.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            transforms into
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_63.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            without alteration of cardinal number. Consequently
          %p
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_64.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mover
                      %mi M
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo ¯
                  %mo =
                  %mover
                    %mover
                      %mi N
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{\overline{M}} = \overline{\overline{N}}
            \.
          %p
            The converse of the theorem results from the remark that between the elements of
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_65.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            and the different units of its cardinal number
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_66.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            a reciprocally univocal (or bi-univocal) relation of correspondence subsists. For, as we saw,
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_67.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mover
                      %mi M
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{\overline{M}}
            grows, so to speak, out of
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_68.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            in such a way that from every element
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_69.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi w
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w
            of
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_70.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            a special unit of
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_71.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            arises. Thus we can say that
          %p
            (9)
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_72.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo ∼
                  %mover
                    %mover
                      %mi M
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M \sim \overline{\overline{M}}
          %p
            In the same way
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_73.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo ∼
                    %mover
                      %mover
                        %mi N
                        %mo ¯
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N \sim \overline{\overline{N}}
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_74.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mover
                        %mi M
                        %mo ¯
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo =
                    %mover
                      %mover
                        %mi N
                        %mo ¯
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{\overline{M}} = \overline{\overline{N}}
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_75.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mo ∼
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M \sim N
          %p
            We will mention the following theorem, which results immediately from the conception of equival ence. If
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_76.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_77.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_78.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_79.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M'
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_80.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N'
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_81.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P'
          %p
            %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_82.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo ∼
                  %mi M
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M \sim M'
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_83.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo ∼
                    %mi N
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N \sim N'
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_9f783515b44df5fc79f83e55edd7d54b06bb3c28_84.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo ∼
                    %mi P
